1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,420
In this lesson, we'll solve triangles that
don't have a right angle, using the law of

2
00:00:05,420 --> 00:00:05,803
sines.

3
00:00:06,933 --> 00:00:12,821
We label a triangle with angles A, B, C
and the sides opposite them a, b, c.

4
00:00:13,271 --> 00:00:17,175
Right triangle trig no longer applies, so
we need new tools.

5
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:24,636
The law of sines says a over sine A equals
b over sine B equals c over sine C.

6
00:00:25,086 --> 00:00:28,755
Each side is proportional to the sine of
its opposite angle.

7
00:00:29,900 --> 00:00:35,647
Suppose A is forty degrees, B is sixty
degrees, and a is ten. Then C is eighty

8
00:00:35,647 --> 00:00:36,236
degrees.

9
00:00:36,686 --> 00:00:42,855
b equals ten sine sixty over sine forty,
about thirteen point five. c equals ten

10
00:00:42,855 --> 00:00:47,097
sine eighty over sine forty, about fifteen
point three.

11
00:00:48,233 --> 00:00:53,456
Given two sides and an angle not between
them, there may be zero, one or two

12
00:00:53,456 --> 00:00:55,999
triangles. That's the ambiguous case.

13
00:00:56,449 --> 00:01:02,849
Say A is thirty degrees, a is six, and b
is ten. Sine B equals ten sine thirty over

14
00:01:02,849 --> 00:01:04,854
six, which is five sixths.

15
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:11,404
Then B is about fifty six point four
degrees, or one eighty minus that, one

16
00:01:11,404 --> 00:01:13,637
twenty three point six degrees.

17
00:01:14,087 --> 00:01:19,058
Both leave room for a positive angle C, so
there are two different triangles.

18
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:25,704
Your turn. A is fifty degrees, B is
seventy degrees, and c is twelve. Find a.

19
00:01:26,154 --> 00:01:27,690
Pause the video and try it.

20
00:01:28,140 --> 00:01:34,171
C is sixty degrees. a equals twelve sine
fifty over sine sixty, about ten point

21
00:01:34,171 --> 00:01:34,476
six.

22
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:40,510
In this lesson, we'll use the law of
cosines, for when the law of sines can't

23
00:01:40,510 --> 00:01:41,275
get started.

24
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:47,349
c squared equals a squared plus b squared
minus two a b cosine C.

25
00:01:47,799 --> 00:01:51,469
The same pattern works for any side and
its opposite angle.

26
00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:58,275
Given a equals eight, b equals eleven, and
C equals thirty seven degrees, find c.

27
00:01:58,725 --> 00:02:04,744
c squared equals sixty four plus one
twenty one minus one seventy six cosine

28
00:02:04,744 --> 00:02:10,842
thirty seven, about forty four point four.
So c is about six point six seven.

29
00:02:11,967 --> 00:02:17,713
Given all three sides, solve for an angle.
Sides five, seven, and nine: find the

30
00:02:17,713 --> 00:02:19,796
largest angle, opposite nine.

31
00:02:20,246 --> 00:02:26,382
Cosine C equals twenty five plus forty
nine minus eighty one, over seventy, which

32
00:02:26,382 --> 00:02:31,382
is negative one tenth. C is about ninety
five point seven degrees.

33
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:38,811
If C is ninety degrees, cosine C is zero,
and the formula becomes a squared plus b

34
00:02:38,811 --> 00:02:40,735
squared equals c squared.

35
00:02:41,185 --> 00:02:46,572
So the law of cosines is the Pythagorean
theorem, corrected for angles that aren't

36
00:02:46,572 --> 00:02:46,966
right.

37
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:53,237
Two ships leave port. One travels twenty
miles, the other thirty miles, with fifty

38
00:02:53,237 --> 00:02:54,991
degrees between their paths.

39
00:02:55,441 --> 00:03:00,839
Their distance apart is the square root of
four hundred plus nine hundred minus

40
00:03:00,839 --> 00:03:04,529
twelve hundred cosine fifty, about twenty
three miles.

41
00:03:05,667 --> 00:03:10,189
Your turn. Sides three, four and six. Find
the angle opposite six.

42
00:03:10,639 --> 00:03:12,175
Pause the video and try it.

43
00:03:12,625 --> 00:03:18,684
Cosine C equals nine plus sixteen minus
thirty six, over twenty four, negative

44
00:03:18,684 --> 00:03:23,655
eleven twenty fourths. C is about one
hundred seventeen degrees.

45
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:29,579
In this lesson, we'll find the area of any
triangle, even without its height.

46
00:03:30,700 --> 00:03:35,948
The height to side b is a sine C. So area
equals one half a b sine C.

47
00:03:36,398 --> 00:03:39,641
Two sides and the angle between them are
all you need.

48
00:03:40,767 --> 00:03:44,671
Sides seven and ten with an included angle
of forty degrees.

49
00:03:45,121 --> 00:03:51,051
Area equals one half times seventy times
sine forty, about twenty two point five.

50
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:56,787
With all three sides, use Heron's formula.
Let s be half the perimeter.

51
00:03:57,237 --> 00:04:04,094
Then area equals the square root of s
times s minus a, times s minus b, times s

52
00:04:04,094 --> 00:04:04,789
minus c.

53
00:04:05,933 --> 00:04:08,899
Sides five, six and seven. s equals nine.

54
00:04:09,349 --> 00:04:15,127
Area equals the square root of nine times
four times three times two, which is the

55
00:04:15,127 --> 00:04:19,567
square root of two hundred sixteen, about
fourteen point seven.

56
00:04:20,700 --> 00:04:26,190
A triangular lot has sides one hundred
twenty and ninety feet, with a sixty five

57
00:04:26,190 --> 00:04:27,975
degree angle between them.

58
00:04:28,425 --> 00:04:34,072
Area equals one half times one twenty
times ninety times sine sixty five, about

59
00:04:34,072 --> 00:04:37,790
four thousand eight hundred ninety four
square feet.

60
00:04:38,933 --> 00:04:44,125
Your turn. Find the area of a triangle
with sides six, eight and ten using

61
00:04:44,125 --> 00:04:45,248
Heron's formula.

62
00:04:45,698 --> 00:04:47,234
Pause the video and try it.

63
00:04:47,684 --> 00:04:53,174
s is twelve, so the area is the square
root of twelve times six times four times

64
00:04:53,174 --> 00:04:54,959
two, which is twenty four.

65
00:04:56,100 --> 00:05:00,900
In this lesson, we'll meet vectors,
quantities with both size and direction.

66
00:05:02,033 --> 00:05:06,961
A vector is an arrow. Its length is the
magnitude, and where it points is the

67
00:05:06,961 --> 00:05:07,601
direction.

68
00:05:08,051 --> 00:05:11,337
Velocity and force are vectors;
temperature is not.

69
00:05:12,467 --> 00:05:17,245
A vector from the origin to three, four
has component form three, four.

70
00:05:17,695 --> 00:05:22,943
Its magnitude is the square root of three
squared plus four squared, which is five.

71
00:05:24,067 --> 00:05:29,678
Add vectors by adding components: three,
four plus one, negative two equals four,

72
00:05:29,678 --> 00:05:29,955
two.

73
00:05:30,405 --> 00:05:35,875
Geometrically, place them tip to tail.
Multiplying by a scalar stretches: two

74
00:05:35,875 --> 00:05:38,149
times three, four is six, eight.

75
00:05:39,267 --> 00:05:43,704
The direction angle comes from tangent
theta equals y over x.

76
00:05:44,154 --> 00:05:49,933
For three, four, theta is inverse tangent
of four thirds, about fifty three point

77
00:05:49,933 --> 00:05:50,789
one degrees.

78
00:05:51,933 --> 00:05:57,935
A unit vector has length one: divide a
vector by its magnitude. And i is one,

79
00:05:57,935 --> 00:06:00,040
zero, while j is zero, one.

80
00:06:00,490 --> 00:06:06,896
A force of fifty newtons at thirty degrees
is fifty cosine thirty i plus fifty sine

81
00:06:06,896 --> 00:06:11,605
thirty j, about forty three point three i
plus twenty five j.

82
00:06:12,733 --> 00:06:16,339
Your turn. Find the magnitude of negative
five, twelve.

83
00:06:16,789 --> 00:06:18,325
Pause the video and try it.

84
00:06:18,775 --> 00:06:23,041
The square root of twenty five plus one
forty four, which is thirteen.

85
00:06:24,166 --> 00:06:29,163
In this lesson, we'll multiply vectors
with the dot product, and use it to find

86
00:06:29,163 --> 00:06:29,606
angles.

87
00:06:30,733 --> 00:06:36,962
The dot product of a one, a two and b one,
b two is a one b one plus a two b two.

88
00:06:37,412 --> 00:06:42,890
It's a number, not a vector. Three, four
dot two, negative one is six minus four,

89
00:06:42,890 --> 00:06:43,769
which is two.

90
00:06:44,899 --> 00:06:50,489
The dot product also equals the magnitudes
times cosine of the angle between them.

91
00:06:50,939 --> 00:06:57,381
So cosine theta equals u dot v over the
magnitude of u times the magnitude of v.

92
00:06:58,499 --> 00:07:03,551
If the dot product is zero, the angle is
ninety degrees: the vectors are

93
00:07:03,551 --> 00:07:04,323
orthogonal.

94
00:07:04,773 --> 00:07:09,740
Two, three and negative six, four:
negative twelve plus twelve is zero, so

95
00:07:09,740 --> 00:07:11,216
they're perpendicular.

96
00:07:12,366 --> 00:07:18,993
The projection of u onto v is the shadow
of u along v: u dot v over the magnitude

97
00:07:18,993 --> 00:07:20,793
of v squared, times v.

98
00:07:21,243 --> 00:07:25,637
It splits a force into the part along a
direction and the part across it.

99
00:07:26,766 --> 00:07:28,942
Work equals force dot displacement.

100
00:07:29,392 --> 00:07:34,607
Pulling a sled with force thirty, ten
newtons over a displacement of twenty,

101
00:07:34,607 --> 00:07:37,627
zero meters does six hundred joules of
work.

102
00:07:38,766 --> 00:07:42,478
Your turn. Find the angle between one,
zero and one, one.

103
00:07:42,928 --> 00:07:44,464
Pause the video and try it.

104
00:07:44,914 --> 00:07:50,226
Cosine theta equals one over root two, so
theta is forty five degrees.

105
00:07:51,366 --> 00:07:56,430
In this lesson, we'll write complex
numbers using trigonometry, which makes

106
00:07:56,430 --> 00:07:57,915
multiplying them easy.

107
00:07:59,033 --> 00:08:02,702
Plot a plus b i at the point a, b in the
complex plane.

108
00:08:03,152 --> 00:08:07,781
The horizontal axis is real, the vertical
axis is imaginary.

109
00:08:08,899 --> 00:08:14,512
The modulus r is the distance from the
origin: the square root of a squared plus

110
00:08:14,512 --> 00:08:15,214
b squared.

111
00:08:15,664 --> 00:08:19,717
The argument theta is the angle from the
positive real axis.

112
00:08:20,866 --> 00:08:25,261
Then z equals r times cosine theta plus i
sine theta.

113
00:08:25,711 --> 00:08:32,286
For one plus i root three, r is two and
theta is pi over three, so z equals two

114
00:08:32,286 --> 00:08:36,697
times cosine pi over three plus i sine pi
over three.

115
00:08:37,833 --> 00:08:41,694
To multiply, multiply the moduli and add
the arguments.

116
00:08:42,144 --> 00:08:48,357
Two at pi over three times three at pi
over six equals six at pi over two, which

117
00:08:48,357 --> 00:08:49,056
is six i.

118
00:08:50,199 --> 00:08:53,997
To divide, divide the moduli and subtract
the arguments.

119
00:08:54,447 --> 00:09:00,800
Eight at five pi over six divided by two
at pi over three equals four at pi over

120
00:09:00,800 --> 00:09:02,468
two, which is four i.

121
00:09:03,599 --> 00:09:07,333
Your turn. Write negative two plus two i
in trig form.

122
00:09:07,783 --> 00:09:09,319
Pause the video and try it.

123
00:09:09,769 --> 00:09:13,182
r is two root two and theta is three pi
over four.

124
00:09:14,300 --> 00:09:18,289
In this lesson, we'll find powers and
roots of complex numbers.

125
00:09:19,433 --> 00:09:26,489
De Moivre's theorem: z to the n equals r
to the n times cosine n theta plus i sine

126
00:09:26,489 --> 00:09:27,177
n theta.

127
00:09:27,627 --> 00:09:31,019
Raise the modulus to the power, multiply
the angle.

128
00:09:32,167 --> 00:09:37,201
Find one plus i, to the eighth. r is root
two, theta is pi over four.

129
00:09:37,651 --> 00:09:43,324
Root two to the eighth is sixteen, and
eight times pi over four is two pi. So the

130
00:09:43,324 --> 00:09:44,585
answer is sixteen.

131
00:09:45,733 --> 00:09:51,024
Every nonzero complex number has exactly n
distinct nth roots.

132
00:09:51,474 --> 00:09:58,478
They have modulus r to the one over n, and
angles theta plus two pi k, over n, for k

133
00:09:58,478 --> 00:10:00,562
from zero to n minus one.

134
00:10:01,700 --> 00:10:06,948
Find the cube roots of eight. Eight has r
equals eight and theta equals zero.

135
00:10:07,398 --> 00:10:13,471
The roots have modulus two and angles
zero, two pi over three and four pi over

136
00:10:13,471 --> 00:10:19,155
three: two, negative one plus i root
three, and negative one minus i root

137
00:10:19,155 --> 00:10:19,622
three.

138
00:10:20,767 --> 00:10:26,356
The nth roots of one, the roots of unity,
sit evenly around the unit circle.

139
00:10:26,806 --> 00:10:32,178
The fourth roots of unity are one, i,
negative one and negative i, forming a

140
00:10:32,178 --> 00:10:32,673
square.

141
00:10:33,800 --> 00:10:36,765
Your turn. Compute two at pi over six,
cubed.

142
00:10:37,215 --> 00:10:38,751
Pause the video and try it.

143
00:10:39,201 --> 00:10:41,527
Eight at pi over two, which is eight i.

144
